#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073584 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fbb6558-01d5-5d08-8e84-64077353b2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Perpendicular Bisectors"}] :question/id #uuid "d49eb5a2-baaf-527f-bfa7-12b2befa865a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2609" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents a perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$?\n\n![](images/90/90e49cf78c7fb86b6f5a8c33b3e5bd28279248f791757a2351ddb86296d79d72.png)" :question/worked-solution "A perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$:\n\n- must split $\\overline{AB}$ in half, and\n- must be perpendicular to $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nAmong the given options, only diagram IV shows a perpendicular bisector to the line segment $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nNotice that in diagrams I, II and III we have bisectors but they are not perpendicular to $\\overline{AB}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073585 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cda2e87-1a65-5075-96fd-98a8a4fd7d7a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Perpendicular Bisector Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "01b53ce1-80d9-530a-bfba-cfd500b37f9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2610" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ in the figure below given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$.\n\n![](images/2d/2dea948c7ab1cf3bb7f543c651d98f23cda8531bbec8cc44e12736c3f04f04c3.png)" :question/worked-solution "By the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment. Hence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nDA &= DB \\\\\nx + 5 &= 2x \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073586 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e57b78-5159-51bc-aeaf-9530f641262b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Converse of the Perpendicular Bisector Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "ab4fe8cf-3651-5a1f-a796-546892cb0178" :question/math-academy-id "e-2613" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ in the figure below given that $AD=BD$ and $AE=BE$.\n\n![](images/e5/e58ea5aadf9075a8b1d7a3de3a3c82e88eb3b47281cf75134631d3a153d42127.png)" :question/worked-solution "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment.\n\nHere, the points $D$ and $E$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}$. Therefore, $D$ and $E$ both lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nIn particular, we have that $AC=BC$. We can use this equation to solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= BC \\\\\n3x + 2 &= 5x - 2 \\\\\n4 &= 2x \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
